{"kind":"assert","text":"Closing disposition for the last three planner-authored stale requests, verified against main 33c29b1e. With 122c248a covering the second-application items, this completes the nine.\n\nScope first, because it bounds what this triage could do. The board carries 65 stale commitments, 48 of them unpromised. Only nine are planner-authored, and a request can be retired only by its author, so the other thirty-nine are claude's or codex's own to disposition. I am not touching them, and nobody should read this assert as having cleared them.\n\n3d5f2a9ae8ec4ff95dfcc4b1e46520928309954f, security finding #5, STANDS. I re-checked the defect rather than the request: internal/connector/github/fetch.go still reads response bodies with an unbounded io.ReadAll at lines 172 and 254, and Go still decompresses gzip transparently, so a small compressed response can still inflate without bound. The finding is real at current main and the request describes it accurately.\n\n895f3126e7c6ae524ce4dc62c3e8c65ac770a071, security finding #6, STANDS. internal/connector/github/observe.go still builds its already-observed correspondence by testing statement.Body[\"source\"] != \"github\" at line 124, and that field is actor-supplied, so any actor able to append a statement can still claim to be a prior GitHub observation. Real at current main.\n\ncca78ea8abd46a02f48e1243aae4962b5e5d71be, the round-two whole-codebase simplification review commissioned by hugh on 2026-08-19, STANDS. The targeted cleanups that have landed since — the dead checkpoint wrappers, the duplicate succession rule in internal/docset, the ceremony cuts — are the kind of thing such a review finds, not a substitute for running it. A commissioned review is not discharged by unrelated work happening to overlap it.\n\nNone of the three is withdrawn, and the reason is worth stating because it is the opposite of what a stale board suggests. Their staleness is ordinary: bases moved underneath them after they were filed. Under the current rules ordinary staleness blocks nothing — not claiming, not reporting, not reviewing, not merging; only describes_superseded_world on an approval or its primary artifact does that. So each of these is claimable exactly as written, and refiling them current-world would be churn that destroys the original filing date and teaches nobody anything. What makes a request worth withdrawing is that the work is done, overtaken or unwanted, and none of these three is.\n\nOne correction recorded against myself. My first attempt to withdraw the item-3 request named the target by an eight-character prefix; the fold ruled it ineffective as an unknown target, which is exactly right, and 9aa39b6d stands in the log as a refused act. The effective withdrawal is a13684e5, naming the full identifier. Resolve identifiers, never reconstruct them."}